.Dd Jul 13, 2026
.Dt XTC_CHASH 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_chash_create ,
.Nm xtc_chash_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_chash_get ,
.Nm xtc_chash_insert ,
.Nm xtc_chash_remove ,
.Nm xtc_chash_size
.Nd RCU-protected concurrent hash table
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_chash.h
.In xtc_rcu.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_chash_create "xtc_chash_cmp_fn cmp" "xtc_chash_hash_fn hash" "size_t initial_capacity" "xtc_chash_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_chash_destroy "xtc_chash_t *h"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_chash_get "xtc_chash_t *h" "const void *key" "void **out_value"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_chash_insert "xtc_chash_t *h" "void *key" "void *value" "void **out_old_value"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_chash_remove "xtc_chash_t *h" "const void *key" "void **out_value"
.Ft size_t
.Fn xtc_chash_size "const xtc_chash_t *h"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_chash
is a chained hash table built on
.Xr xtc_rcu 3
so that lookups are wait-free -- no lock, no atomic
compare-exchange, just acquire-ordered loads down a bucket chain --
while inserts and removes still support many concurrent writers.
It is the primary consumer motivating
.Nm xtc_rcu Ns 's
existence in this tree.
.Pp
Keys and values are caller-owned
.Vt void *
pointers stored verbatim, the same convention as
.Xr xtc_chan 3
and
.Xr xtc_pdict 3 :
the table never copies a key or value and never frees one.  A
caller-supplied comparator
.Fa cmp
(the
.Xr qsort 3
convention -- only the
.Dv == 0
vs.\&
.Dv != 0
distinction matters here) and hash function
.Fa hash
make the table key-type-agnostic.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_chash_create
allocates a table with
.Fa initial_capacity
buckets, rounded up to the next power of two
.Pq 0 defaults to 16 .
.Fn xtc_chash_destroy
frees the table's own bookkeeping; it does not free caller-owned
keys/values and is not safe to call concurrently with any other
call on the same table -- quiesce first, exactly as
.Xr xtc_rcu 3 Ns 's
.Fn xtc_rcu_fini
requires.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_chash_get
must be called inside the caller's own
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_lock Ns / Ns Fn xtc_rcu_read_unlock
bracket
.Pq nesting is fine, see Xr xtc_rcu 3 .
On a hit it writes the value pointer to
.Fa *out_value
and returns
.Dv XTC_OK ;
the pointer is valid only until the caller's read-side ends.  On a
miss it returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_chash_insert
and
.Fn xtc_chash_remove
take the read-side internally for their own traversal; the caller
does not need an outer read-side to call them, though nesting inside
one is harmless.
.Fn xtc_chash_insert
replaces any existing entry for an equal key, writing the old value
pointer to
.Fa *out_old_value
.Pq NULL for a fresh insert; the argument itself may be NULL .
It may trigger a grow-only resize.
.Fn xtc_chash_remove
writes the removed value pointer to
.Fa *out_value
.Pq may be NULL
and returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
on a miss.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_chash_size
returns the approximate live entry count -- exact with no concurrent
writers, a fresh-enough estimate otherwise.
.Sh CONCURRENCY AND RECLAMATION
Writers serialize through a fixed set of 64 stripe mutexes; a key's
stripe is derived from its bucket index in the CURRENT array (so two
keys in the same bucket always take the same stripe lock -- a
correctness requirement, not just an optimisation), and two writers
touching different buckets that happen to share a stripe only pay a
false-sharing serialization cost, never a correctness one.
.Pp
A removed node is unlinked by overwriting its predecessor's link with
a single release-ordered store of the removed node's own
.Pq unchanged
successor -- the node's own
.Va next
field is never touched, so a reader already parked inside it can
finish walking the rest of the chain exactly as it stood when it
arrived.  The node itself is handed to
.Fn xtc_rcu_retire ,
never freed synchronously: a concurrent reader that already loaded a
pointer to it keeps a fully valid node until it leaves its
read-side.
.Pp
A grow claims every stripe lock
.Pq blocking writers, never readers ,
duplicates every live node into a freshly allocated, larger array,
publishes the new array with a release store, and retires the old
array and its original nodes via
.Fn xtc_rcu_retire .
A reader that loaded the old array before the swap keeps working
against a fully valid, if smaller, table until it leaves its
read-side.
.Pp
This module never calls
.Fn xtc_rcu_synchronize
itself; as with every other
.Xr xtc_rcu 3
consumer, actual reclamation of retired nodes happens on the
caller's, a test's, or an application-installed reaper's schedule.
.Sh RESIZE
.Nm xtc_chash
is
.Em grow-only .
The table doubles its bucket array past a 75% load factor and never
shrinks; a table that grows to a high-water mark and then drains
stays at that array size until
.Fn xtc_chash_destroy .
A real shrink would need the same RCU-swap machinery run in reverse
plus a low-load-factor trigger; deferred until a workload needs it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv XTC_OK
on success;
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL table/key or missing comparator/hash at create;
.Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND
on a get/remove miss;
.Dv XTC_E_NOMEM
on allocation failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static int
str_cmp(const void *a, const void *b)
{
	return strcmp((const char *)a, (const char *)b);
}

static uint64_t
str_hash(const void *key)
{
	const char *s = key;
	uint64_t h = 1469598103934665603ULL;
	for (; *s != '\e0'; s++) { h ^= (uint8_t)*s; h *= 1099511628211ULL; }
	return h;
}

xtc_chash_t *h;
xtc_chash_create(str_cmp, str_hash, 64, &h);
xtc_chash_insert(h, "answer", (void *)(intptr_t)42, NULL);

xtc_rcu_read_lock();
void *v;
if (xtc_chash_get(h, "answer", &v) == XTC_OK)
	printf("%ld\en", (intptr_t)v);
xtc_rcu_read_unlock();

xtc_chash_destroy(h);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_rcu 3 ,
.Xr xtc_cskip 3 ,
.Xr xtc_chan 3 ,
.Xr xtc_pdict 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
